WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.509
Superhero movies crowded 2018's film release
schedule, not to mention the growing number

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:10.240
of superhero television shows, and of course
the comics upon which everything else has

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:10.740
been based.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.640
And with more superhero media comes a higher
superhero body count.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.060
In the season 3 finale of DC’s Legends of
Tomorrow, the time-hopping team lost the guy

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.830
who brought them all together: the former
Time Master Rip Hunter, played by Arthur Darvill.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.440
“The name’s Rip Hunter.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.100
I’m from East London.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.040
Oh, and THE FUTURE!”

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.040
Hunter had spent the entire third season obsessed
with the demon Mallus, to the point where

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.400
he was even discharged from his job as a Time
Master.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.500
It makes sense, then, that he met his fate
battling against that same demon, detonating

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.880
a Time Core in order to save his friends,
and sacrificing his life in the process.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.920
Unfortunately, that explosion only wounded
Mallus, but the Legends were eventually able

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.280
to defeat the demon thanks to a 12-foot teddy
bear.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.180
No, really.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.980
"I luh-luh-love you."

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.940
Considering that the entire show is built
around time travel and the occasional alternate

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.740
reality, it wouldn’t be much of a stretch
to see Darvil back in action in a future season.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.960
In the meantime, though, the Legends will
have to fend for themselves.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.660
R.I.P., Rip.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:23.080
Avengers: Infinity War brought a lot of death
to the Marvel Cinematic Universe, claiming

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.820
a higher superhero body count than any other
MCU film to date.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.600
He certainly wouldn’t be the last, but the
major character with the dubious honor of

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.240
dying first was Heimdall, as played by Idris
Elba.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.380
Having helped shepherd the surviving Asgardians
to safety at the end of Thor: Ragnarok, Heimdall

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.900
was on board the refugee ship when Thanos
and his Black Order attacked.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.900
At the start of the film, he’s already seriously
injured, and in no position to help Thor,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.620
Loki, and the Hulk in their initial skirmish
with Thanos.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.200
At least, not at first.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.700
Presumably hoping that Bruce Banner would
warn the heroes of Earth that Thanos was on

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.689
his way, Heimdall used the last of his strength
to activate the Bifrost and send the Hulk

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:04.540
back to Earth… right before Thanos impaled
him.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.260
By the time Avengers: Infinity War rolled
around, Loki had already “died” twice,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.980
only to prove that he was a little more resilient
than the rest of the Asgardians assumed.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.280
This time, though, it’s tough to imagine
him pulling off that same trick again.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.460
After handing over the Tesseract — which
turned out to be an Infinity Stone — it

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.769
seemed like Loki might go back to his old
tricks and ally himself with Thanos, just

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:28.700
like he had back in the first Avengers film.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.480
The act tricked everyone, including Thor,
but it wasn’t enough to stop Thanos.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.380
In the end, the Mad Titan easily choked the
life out of the God of Mischief.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.040
Considering all the innocents who died at
Loki’s hands during the Battle of Manhattan,

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.790
not to mention the murder of his biological
father, attempted genocide of the Frost Giants,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:47.680
and various other crimes...

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.340
“He transformed himself into a snake and
he knows that I love snakes so I went to pick

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.159
up the snake to admire it and he transformed
back into himself and went BAH it’s me!

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.220
and then he stabbed me.”

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.120
….it’s pretty easy to argue that Loki
doesn’t belong on any list of fallen heroes.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.560
That said, his actions at the end of Ragnarok
and a noble sacrifice in Infinity War show

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.319
that he’s more than just another supervillain.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:14.049
Of all the deaths doled out by Thanos in Avengers:
Infinity War, the only one that he showed

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:17.260
any regret for was the murder of his adopted
daughter Gamora.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.040
After she revealed the location of the Soul
Stone to stop Thanos from torturing Nebula,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.240
Gamora led Thanos to the planet Vormir.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.920
On that desolate world, a ghostly apparition
of the Red Skull appeared to them, and explained

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.720
that in order to gain the Soul Stone, Thanos
had to sacrifice the thing he loved most.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.900
“You failed… do you want to know why?

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.300
Because you love nothing.”

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.260
Much to her own surprise, it turned out that
the only thing Thanos loved was Gamora herself,

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:52.809
and while she tried to kill herself before
Thanos could, he threw her from a cliff to

00:03:52.809 --> 00:03:53.800
claim his prize.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.659
As someone who has an Infinity Stone embedded
in his head that’s keeping him alive, the

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.500
Vision was on the chopping block from the
start of Infinity War.

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:06.619
Admittedly, every single being in the universe
was in danger in that movie, but for Vision,

00:04:06.619 --> 00:04:10.450
the threat was pretty direct, and involved
everyone else doing their best to protect

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.180
him including Captain America.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.760
Once he learns of Thanos' plans, Vision insists
the only choice was to let Scarlet Witch use

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:22.669
her powers to destroy the Mind Stone, but
despite a group of Marvel Universe super-geniuses

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:25.480
working on it, there was no way to get it
out safely.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.360
When Thanos arrived in Wakanda with every
other Infinity Stone under his command, Scarlet

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.520
Witch destroys the Mind Stone, killing Vision
in the process… only for Thanos to use the

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.460
powers of the Time Stone to put Vision back
together -- so that he could take him out

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.100
in his own, special, mind-stone-extracting
way.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.920
Avengers: Infinity War ended with a cliffhanger
so dark, it made Empire Strikes Back say,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.420
"Dude.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.040
Lighten up."

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.400
It probably goes without saying, but one of
the comics that heavily influenced Infinity

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.900
War was Marvel’s 1991 miniseries, Infinity
Gauntlet.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:01.820
In the comic, the story begins when Thanos
gathers all six Infinity Stones and, with

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.349
a snap of his fingers, kills half the sentient
beings in the universe including a whole lot

00:05:06.349 --> 00:05:10.460
of superheroes, which kept the cast to a relatively
manageable size.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:15.860
Infinity War saved that moment for the conclusion,
and it's devastating, and far more horrific

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.020
than the source material.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:19.780
In the comics, people just blinked out of
existence.

00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:25.260
In Infinity War, they feel what's coming before
it hits, and they're slowly erased, inch by

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.760
inch, until there's nothing left.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.580
By the time the credits roll we watch Bucky,
Falcon, Scarlet Witch, Black Panther, Spider-Man,

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.669
Groot, Star-Lord, Drax, Mantis, and Doctor
Strange all get erased from the canvas of

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:38.160
reality.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.140
In the film's one and only post-credits scene,
we see similar fates befall Maria Hill and

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:43.840
Nick Fury.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.560
Considering that there are already confirmed
sequels in the works for Guardians of the

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.490
Galaxy, Black Panther, and Spider-Man: Homecoming,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:52.980
“What the F?”

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.680
it's a safe bet that not all of these dusty
deaths will stick, but we won’t know for

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.270
sure until Avengers 4 finally hits theaters.

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:05.270
If there were an Oscar for misleading trailers,
Deadpool 2 would be bringing home at least

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:05.980
one statue.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.220
In spite of being featured in at least one
action scene in the ads, Bedlam, an obscure

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.590
mutant played in the film by Terry Crews,
was the first of Wade Wilson's X-Force to

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:16.360
kick the bucket.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.100
He even did it before he got to throw a single
punch.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.539
In order to protect an imprisoned teenage
mutant from the vengeful, time-hopping Cable,

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:28.919
Deadpool formed his own X-Force team with
designs on an air drop to a prisoner transport

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:29.500
convoy.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.039
Unfortunately for this new crop of heroes,
high-speed winds blew the team off course

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:38.740
and save for Deadpool and Domino the whole
roster of X-Force ended up being killed.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.740
Bedlam was blown face-first into the windshield
of a public bus.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:47.860
“NO!

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.560
Tripping Motherf------- Billies!”

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.750
In the darkly hilarious carnage that followed,
the most surprising death was Shatterstar.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.840
A long-time X-Force member in the comics,
Shatterstar was heavily featured in the trailers,

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.020
but sadly wound up on the wrong end of a helicopter
and got chopped to pieces.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.920
Moviegoers didn’t even get to see his cool
swords!

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.280
They have two blades!

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.620
Next up on X-Force’s list of untimely deaths
was the Vanisher.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.740
He was one of the X-Men’s earliest comic
book villains, but when he showed up (or didn’t)

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:24.240
in Deadpool 2, no one was really sure if Telford
Porter, the teleporting mutant with what might

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.980
be the worst name in comics history, actually
existed.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.031
Eventually, though, it turned out that he
was a full-fledged member of the team, and

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.880
moviegoers got a glimpse at just who was behind
his less-than-visible presence..

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.160
“Maybe the wind can’t blow what it can’t
see.”

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:41.600
“REALLY!?”

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.930
In the comics, Zeitgeist is the leader of
a reality-show celebrity version of X-Force…

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:48.420
for about five minutes.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:52.860
He actually dies in the same issue where he
makes his first appearance, along with most

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.990
of his team, so if nothing else, Deadpool
2 has that bit of true-to-canon accuracy going

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:58.480
for it.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.620
Also accurate: his extremely gross super-power
of barfing acid.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.180
The bad news well, worse news is that we only
get to see that power when Zeitgeist winds

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:09.180
up in a woodchipper.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:15.559
The powerless Peter tries to save his new
teammate, only to get his arm… um acided?

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.780
off by Zeitgeist’s vomit.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:17.940
And die himself.

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:22.120
That’s gross enough to make you long for
helicopter blades or a speeding bus, but at

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.620
least Peter was saved by Deadpool’s time-hopping
mini-adventure at the end.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:31.219
When Deadpool uses Cable's time machine to
undo Vanessa and Peter's deaths in the mid-credits

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:36.180
sequence of Deadpool 2, he also uses it to
correct some more notorious mistakes.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:41.580
X-Men Origins: Wolverine is often considered
a low point for Deadpool fans, mostly because

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:45.920
it turned the famously talkative Wade Wilson
into into a mouthless, murderous Mortal Kombat

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:46.420
ripoff.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.340
As much as you may hate that movie, Ryan Reynolds
has shown us that he probably hates it more,

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:55.760
parodying that movie’s action in Deadpool
2 with some flashy but completely ineffective

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:56.500
sword action.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.540
"Whoo, those bullets are like, super fast."

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:09.570
That still wasn’t enough, though, so once
he gets his hands on some time travel, Deadpool’s

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:14.070
first priority is to head back to 2009 to
fire bullet after bullet into his previous

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:14.660
incarnation.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:20.660
While most of Ant-Man and the Wasp takes place
before the events of Avengers: Infinity War,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.260
the post-credits scenes caught up to the Avengers'
ill-fated battle with Thanos.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.279
In those, we learned that the original Ant-Man
and both women who went by the codename Wasp

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:34.090
were victims of Thanos' infamous "snap,”
leaving only their dust blowing in the breeze.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.860
Before they set their sights on Shatter, the
anti-mutant hate-group called the Purifiers

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.140
were bad, but hadn’t gone as far as murder.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.840
Then, in the 19th episode of the series, that
line is crossed.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:49.380
It started when disgraced Sentinel Agent Jace
Turner joined the Purifiers.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.780
He seemed intent on changing their method,
but after finding the location of the Mutant

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:57.420
Underground’s base, he led an attack with
lethal consequences.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.920
Shatter volunteered to distract the Purifiers,
and while most of their weapons couldn’t

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.360
penetrate his skin, Agent Turner brought heavier
ordinance.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.200
Clearly conflicted but ultimately resolved
to his course, Turner unloaded a machine gun

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.640
at Shatter, killing him.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:17.620
"Elseworlds" the 2018 crossover that ran through
the majority of the CW's Arrowverse series

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:22.759
was highly anticipated for its live-action
debut of Ruby Rose as Batwoman, the role reversals

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.080
of Barry Allen and Oliver Queen, and Superman's
black suit.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.240
"I decide who’s story ends.”

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.580
“ … I like you better in blue"

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.740
The 
episodes immediately preceding the crossover

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:39.560
for each respective participating series ended
with the same brief scene of destruction:

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:43.400
the lifeless bodies of superheroes scattered
around a war-torn landscape.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:48.360
Most of the corpses' faces were hidden, but
judging by the costumes, the dead heroes from

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:54.700
Earth-90 included the Ray, Hawkman, Hawkgirl,
Firestorm, Huntress, Captain Cold, and Stargirl.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.720
The only survivor of Earth-90's heroes was
the Flash, who flees his world before the

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.060
Monitor can destroy it.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.360
We don't know if the older Flash survived
the crossover.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.340
After he contacts the heroes of Earth-1, the
Earth-90 Flash fights beside them when they

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:11.760
launch a brief and futile attack on the Monitor.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:16.500
With just a motion of the Monitor's hand,
Earth-90 Flash disappears in a cloud of smoke

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:17.840
and never reappears.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:23.160
Monitor uses similar powers on other heroes
without killing them, but since Earth-90 no

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:26.750
longer exists it's unclear where Monitor sent
the older Flash.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.980
"Go find someone else to prank, douchebag"

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.620
Probably the most blatant way the animated
film Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse differed

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:41.460
from the comic book event that inspired it
is the film doesn't have much of a body count.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:46.160
Marvel's Spider-Verse crossover didn't just
introduce loads of alternate reality versions

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.820
of Spider-Man it killed a whole bunch of them,
too.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.740
Obviously Into the Spider-Verse wanted a family-friendly
appeal that would be spoiled by the brutal

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:58.500
murders of dozens of spider-themed heroes,
but there was one victim among its spiders.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.420
Peter Parker is dispatched by the Kingpin
early in the film.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:06.170
Miles Morales and the other Spider heroes
rally at the home of Peter's Aunt May, where

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.280
we learn the late Parker kept his own "Spider-Cave"
below May's backyard shed.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.300
Parker's death partly mirrored the character's
fate in the now-defunct Ultimate line of Marvel's

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:15.800
comics.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:20.160
The Ultimate version of Peter Parker died
while fighting the Green Goblin and was replaced

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:21.980
by the new Spider-Man, Miles Morales.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.680
"Thwip and release"
"Thwip and release"

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.900
"Thwip and release"
"You're a natural"

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.740
"Thwip and release"
“Feel the rhythm?”

